{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"660c6325-7869-4bb6-aa32-325db4ae86a5","status":"SUCCESS","response":"I have completed the `lab-09-scalable-structures` as requested.\n\nHere is a summary of the work done:\n- **`scalable_counters.c`**: Created a benchmark comparing a `pthread_mutex_t` locked counter, an `_Atomic` counter, and a padded per-thread counter to demonstrate inter-processor coherence traffic overhead.\n- **`false_sharing.c`**: Authored a test contrasting an array of unpadded atomic counters vs. a cache-line padded array to highlight MESI invalidation costs under false sharing.\n- **`read_mostly.c`**: Wrote a comparison between a standard `pthread_rwlock_t` and a rudimentary epoch-based user-space RCU-like reader-writer pattern to show lockless read advantages.\n- **Build and Test**: Set up a `Makefile` supporting `make test`, verified successfully inside the `aos` Lima VM.\n- **Results**: Captured real performance numbers using `run-in-vm.sh lab-09-scalable-structures capture` (achieving ~0.004s for per-thread vs ~1.17s for locked counters, and highlighting false sharing delays).\n- **Documentation**: Updated `README.md` to `status: solid` following your Obsidian vault constraints (no emojis, explicit sentence wrapping, matching metadata structure) incorporating explanations, VM captured outputs, experiments, and questions.\n- **Version Control**: Added a clean `.gitignore` (ignoring binaries but keeping `expected-output.txt`), formatted the files, and created a standard git commit (`feat(aos): add lab-09 on scalable kernel structures`).\n- **Reporting**: Saved the final completion report to `~/github/career-ops/library/01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/CS6210-AOS/crew/runner/reports/lab-09-scalable-structures.md`.\n","duration_seconds":253.677425,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":161145,"output_tokens":15433,"thinking_tokens":4455,"cache_read_tokens":1308579,"total_tokens":176578}}}
